WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 3 Student Video
Unit 1, Lesson 3: Truth

1
00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.490
2,000 years ago, Jesus said, I'm one of the ways, one of the truths, and one

2
00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.730
possible life. If you want to come to the mother or the father through me, that's

3
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.330
cool. Go forth living according to whatever feels right to you.

4
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.650
Okay, you got me. Clearly, Jesus didn't say that.

5
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.410
In fact, he said quite the opposite.

6
00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:27.030
If you look in the New Testament, you will find at least 100 verses that make it

7
00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:32.510
clear that Jesus is the only way to God, the only way to experience an eternal

8
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:37.750
relationship with the Father. Truth is vitally important because eternity

9
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:43.590
is at stake. Now, philosophers use a term that seems fancy, but I think you'll find

10
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.350
it's kind of common sense, what's called the correspondence theory of truth.

11
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.870
And all this means is you have essentially a belief, this is an idea

12
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:59.870
you hold about the world. But then you have the world over here, this is reality.

13
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:04.050
Now, truth is not a belief. Truth is not reality.

14
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.810
Truth is when your belief matches up with reality.

15
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:14.490
That's what we mean by truth. Have you ever noticed how much you base your entire

16
00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:20.070
life down to the details, the daily decisions on what you think is true?

17
00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:20.870
What

18
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.560
if somebody says it's cold outside?

19
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.720
How do you check? Do you know whether that is true or whether that's false?

20
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.240
Well, you could look at a weather app and see what information it gives you, or you

21
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.540
could actually walk outside and you could see it for yourself.

22
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.920
If it is cold outside, then your belief is true.

23
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.980
If it's not cold outside, then the belief is false.

24
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.960
We do this with historical issues, such as the claim that Jesus rose from the grave,

25
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.720
and we do this with scientific matters, such as the claim or the belief that the

26
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.320
universe had a beginning. We actually do this on all sorts of issues.

27
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.260
We hold a variety of beliefs, and then we go see if the world is the way we take it

28
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.500
to be. If our beliefs match up, it's true.

29
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.640
If our beliefs don't, it's false.

30
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.140
But remember to keep this in mind.

31
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:21.220
Our beliefs don't create reality, but rather our beliefs should reflect reality.

32
00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.860
You might be thinking, wait a minute.

33
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.540
When it comes to religious claims, can't you have your beliefs and I have mine?

34
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.240
Aren't your beliefs true for you?

35
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.400
But my religious beliefs are true for me.

36
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.300
Not too long ago, my father was speaking at a leadership event for about 400 high

37
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.920
school students. And in his session, my dad decided to walk out into the audience

38
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.180
and interact with the students. And he started by asking a simple question to a

39
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.900
student that was there. He said, do you think the Bible is true?

40
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.620
And the student jumped in. He goes, yes, I think the Bible is true.

41
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.560
My dad said, do you think it's scientifically and historically accurate in

42
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.420
everything it teaches that we properly understand?

43
00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.880
The student looked back. He instantly said, of course.

44
00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.440
And then my dad asked this simple question back.

45
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.640
He said, why?

46
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.180
And the student had no answer. In fact, my dad went around the room and not a single

47
00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:16.080
student had a thoughtful answer to why he or she believed that the Bible is true.

48
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.660
Well, the session was done and my dad was walking towards the back of the

49
00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.760
auditorium. And the student comes running up to my father.

50
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.940
He goes, Josh, Josh, I know the answer.

51
00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.440
I know why the Bible is true. My dad said, great.

52
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.400
Why is the Bible true? He looks at my father and he says, The Bible is true because

53
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.620
I believe it. My dad said, wait a minute.

54
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.280
The Bible is true because you believe it.

55
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.660
What about a Quran and a Muslim who believes the Quran?

56
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.260
This student looked my dad right back in the eyes and he said, If a Muslim believes

57
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:51.220
the Quran is true, then the Quran is true for him.

58
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.700
Now, my dad gave back to the student a memorable and powerful response.

59
00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.080
He said, here's the difference between me.

60
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.720
And you, you think the Bible is true because you believe it, but I believe the

61
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.760
Bible because I think it is true.

62
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.000
We see nothing is true or false because we believe it.

63
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.260
Our beliefs don't change reality.

64
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:21.380
But given how important truth is, let's hope that our beliefs match up

65
00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:22.180
with reality.

66
00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.000
We've been talking about why truth is important.

67
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.000
But let me share a story about my mom to help make this point really sink in.

68
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.360
So my mom has come a long ways with technology, but she honestly used to be afraid

69
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:37.520
of anything, technologically speaking.

70
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.500
In fact, when my mom gets something new with technology, she would read the

71
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.500
instructions letter by letter and follow it as best as she can.

72
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.240
Well, she got a computer. Up pops something that says, close all

73
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:50.840
windows.

74
00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.660
Well, my mom decides to, and I think you see where this is going.

75
00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.060
Get up from her chair, walk around the house and close all the windows.

76
00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.760
And when she told this story, obviously a little bit embarrassed, we realized that

77
00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:12.620
truth has consequences. In this case, embarrassment, confusion, but in many cases,

78
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.300
confusing truth can lead to devastating consequences.

79
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.940
But when we know truth, we're set free.

80
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.120
So we see a computer. It's created and designed to function a certain way.

81
00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.320
When we use it according to its design, then we're set free.

82
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.440
We can accomplish it. We can accomplish things.

83
00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:34.740
But when we don't know its design, like in the story with my mom, what happens?

84
00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:39.040
Confusion, frustration, and sometimes significant consequences.

85
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.160
I think one of the greatest lies and confusions of our age deals with the question

86
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.780
of what is freedom. Have you ever thought in your life, what is real freedom?

87
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.880
When I ask students to define freedom, typically they'll say to me, freedom is when

88
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.760
you can do whatever you want to do, as long as nobody is telling you how to live

89
00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.560
your life. If it feels good. Do it.

90
00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.300
And you're free. Friends, that's not freedom.

91
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:09.480
Living according to whatever feels good is going to enslave you to your passions.

92
00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:15.940
Real freedom is when we embrace God's design for our life, which is to love Him and

93
00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:21.860
to love other people. That's why Jesus said in John chapter 3 that if we live

94
00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:27.420
according to His design for our life, that we live in truth, our works will be made

95
00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.980
known to the world.

96
00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.260
Real freedom is not living according to whatever we think is right, but embracing

97
00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.480
God's design for our life. Real freedom is when we live in light.

98
00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.140
Bright. Light. Love.

